# Shape of a controller

Derive from `TCRControllerBase`, decorate with `[Authorize]` and `[NoCache]`, and provide two
constructors: a parameterless one for production and a `(IAppSettings, DataContext, ICurrentUser)` one
for tests. Instantiate providers directly in both — there is no DI container.

Routing is **action-based**, not REST: `api/{controller}/{action}/{id}` from `WebApiConfig`. Reads are
`[HttpGet]`, everything else is `[HttpPost]` taking a model. JSON is serialized camelCase, so the
AngularJS side sees `dateEntry`, `userAccountId`, and so on.

Call `CheckModelState()` before persisting, and translate domain exceptions yourself:

```csharp
catch (TimesheetException e)
{
    throw new HttpResponseException(Request.CreateErrorResponse(HttpStatusCode.BadRequest, e.Message));
}
```

# Grid endpoints

A grid action takes a `GridModel` subclass (`IdGridModel`, or a purpose-built search model) and returns
`GridResultModel<T>`. Call `SetupGridParams(model)` first — it lowercases `SortKey`, defaults `SortOrder`
to `"ASC"`, defaults `RecordsPerPage` to 60, and turns an empty `Searchfor` into the literal string
`"null"`, which is why filters are written as `if (model.Searchfor != "null")`.

Sorting is a hand-written `switch` on the lowercased `SortKey`, so **a new sortable column silently
falls back to the default order until you add its `case`**. Project into the grid model *before*
sorting so the `switch` can order by the projected property.

Some grids have their `Skip`/`Take` paging commented out and return every row. Do not copy that into a
new endpoint; page server-side unless the result set is known to be tiny.
